  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nCurrently have upper ridging centered over the TX Gulf Coast with \nwater vapor imagery showing an upper impulse along the AZ/NM \nborder. Not much at the surface to focus on with a broad area of\nmoist east/southeast flow across the Southern Plains. \n\nMid level impulse over the Desert Southwest will start to lift \nnortheast today and by later this afternoon will be moving into the \nCentral Rockies with much of the upper energy moving out across \nwestern KS. Ahead of this wave, most of the area will remain in very \nmoist 850-700mb flow with PW values around 175% of normal. This will \nlead to continued scattered showers and isolated storms through\nmuch of the day, especially for locations along and west of \nI-135. We should see an uptick in convection around 00z as upper\njet max starts to move into western portions of the forecast \narea. The more widespread convection will lift northeast with \nthe upper impulse as Sat morning approaches. \n\nFor Sat, mid/upper energy will be lifting into the Upper Mississippi \nValley with a broad area of upper ridging across southern TX into \nthe Gulf. While widespread rainfall is not expected on Sat, we will \nremain in moist 850-700mb flow with an unseasonably high PW airmass \nin place. This could result in some sct-iso showers and storms \nthroughout the day, especially across the Flint Hills into eastern \nKS. Rain chances will continue to push east Sat night and by early \nSun morning, only far southeast KS should still have precip chances. \nThis will leave dry conditions in place for most of Sun through \nMonday.\n\nBy 00z Tue, there is good model agreement in a robust upper low \ndigging across Southern CA into the Desert Southwest. As the low \nslides east Mon night, low level jet and associated moisture \ntransport will ramp-up, increasing shower and storm chances across \nthe area. High precip chances will remain through Tue as strong 850-\n700mb moisture transport remains across the Plains with the upper \nlow lagging back over NM. Upper low then looks to move out across \nthe Southern Plains for Wed through Thu. Models have not been very \nconsistent with the timing of the low so confidence beyond Tue is \nlow at this time.",
